//test/Pex--------------------    }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ClearContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            arrList.Clear();

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ New_arrListCount is smaller or equal to Old_arrListCount
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            object Old_arrListItemAtIndex = arrList[index];

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_ItemAt_index", Old_arrListItemAtIndex);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ New_arrListCount is equal to Old_arrListCount minus one
        }                
   }
}

The contract Put_RemoveAtContract has a post condition that checks if the Count of the list decreases after the RemoveAt. Unfortunately, there's no example given in the contract's documentation. So the test case for this failed, because no such precondition was specified.
For the ClearContract there's also no example on how to specify the post condition, even though the contract promises that the Count of the list will be 0 after the method called.
This code was auto generated using PexAPIWrapper.
Is anyone familiar with this problem or has any way to specify post-conditions for these contracts?

A: First of all, you cannot directly manipulate the test cases generated by Pex's API to add postconditions. You should add these yourself manually in the test cases and use NUnit's `Assert.AreEqual or Assert.AreSame method (if it makes sense to do so).

Additionally, the contracts in this API do not support adding postconditions. Instead, you need to add invariants. These help to state certain conditions that need to be true at the end of every method execution which does not change the state (remains part of your logic and can therefore be verified automatically).

Postcondition in Pex terminology can be considered as invariants at the beginning of the tests. So, for example, you could start your test like this:

    [PexMethod]
    [PexPostCondition(PexMethod = "ArrayList.Add", Arguments = new object[] {6}, ExpectedException = typeof(ArgumentException))]
    public PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x)
    { 
...

This tells Pex that the Add method with any arguments will throw an exception with the typeof(ArgumentException) if called on arrList. This could be used as a postcondition for your Add method. However, it seems there's still a misunderstanding with regard to postcondition support in Pex API Wrapper.
Further, Pex documentation doesn't have enough detail about postcondition support in its current version. You may find it helpful if you dig deeper into its auto-generated attributes.
For more accurate and detailed information on how testing contracts in PEX, you might want to refer to this official blog:
http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/hh314929(v=vs.110).aspx

Hope this helps.
Joe

A: Pex does not currently support declaring post-conditions. However, you can specify pre-conditions via the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute and verify post-conditions via other appropriate NUnit assertions.
For example:
In the method below, the `PexMethod` attribute with the pre-condition and expectation--------------------	   PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$assert", New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index);
        }
    }      
